[Event "French Defense: the wall and the counterpunch"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/uIXK7n1G/rd263BaC"]

{ Today we learn the French Defense. A solid wall, and a counterpunch always loaded. } 1. e4 { He grabs the middle. Answer one step only: pawn to e6. That is the French. [%cal Ye7e6] } 1... e6 (1... e5 { A fine move, and a whole other world. } 2. Nf3 { That is the open game. Today we build the French wall. }) (1... c5 { The Sicilian. Sharp and famous, but not this lesson. } 2. Nf3 { Another day, another fight. }) 2. d4 { He takes it all. Now hit back: d5, staking your claim. [%cal Yd7d5] } 2... d5 (2... c5 { A Sicilian idea. Here it lets his pawn charge past. } 3. d5 { He owns the middle. In the French, d5 belongs to you. }) (2... Nf6 { The knight runs into the pawns. } 3. e5 Nd5 4. c4 { He chases your knight and grabs the whole board. }) 3. e5 { He locks the middle. Your whole plan is one move: c5, biting the chain at its base. [%csl Rd4][%cal Yc7c5] } 3... c5 { The French counterpunch. His chain hangs from d4. Chew on it. [%cal Gc5d4] } (3... Bd7 { The engine likes this quiet move too. } 4. c3 c5 { And you strike anyway. Often the same house by another door. }) (3... Ne7 { Also fine. The knight often hops to f5 later. } 4. c3 c5 { But this punch comes first, sooner or later. }) (3... Nc6 { The knight blocks your own c pawn. The punch is gone. } 4. Nf3 { He builds in peace while you sit cramped. }) (3... f6 { The other lever, but far too soon. } 4. Bd3 { Your king side is airy and his bishop grins at it. c5 first. }) (3... b6 { Too slow. } 4. c3 { He gets a dream center for free. }) 4. c3 { He props the chain. Pile on: knight to c6 leans on d4. [%cal Yb8c6] } 4... Nc6 (4... Qb6 { Good square, and we are heading there. } 5. Nf3 Nc6 { Same position. The knight and queen just swapped turns. }) (4... cxd4 { You may, but keep the tension a while. } 5. cxd4 { See it: now his queen knight gets the c3 square for free. }) (4... Bd7 { A useful piece later. } 5. Nf3 { But bring the fighters first: knight and queen. }) 5. Nf3 { He guards d4 again. Now the queen: out to b6, a second fist on d4. [%cal Yd8b6] } 5... Qb6 { Queen and knight both glare at d4. He must babysit that pawn forever. [%cal Gb6d4,Gc6d4] } (5... Bd7 { Also a real line. } 6. Be2 Nge7 { Fine chess. Ours sends the queen to bully d4 first. }) (5... Nge7 { The engine's own favourite. f5 is the dream. } 6. Na3 cxd4 7. cxd4 Nf5 { The same song. Learn ours with the queen first. }) (5... Nf6 { Look again: the e5 pawn simply eats that knight. [%cal Re5f6] }) (5... cxd4 { Fine too. } 6. cxd4 { Play the queen to b6 next and we are back in our line. }) 6. Be2 { The modest bishop move. Trade pawns now and open the c column. [%cal Yc5d4] } 6... cxd4 (6... Nh6 { Careful with the order. } 7. Bxh6 gxh6 { He may rip your king side. Trade pawns first, then hop the knight. }) (6... Bd7 { Fine and calm. } 7. O-O { But ask d4 the question before he settles in. }) (6... Nge7 { The knight went out one door too early. } 7. dxc5 Qxc5 8. b4 { His pawns arrive with tempo on your queen. }) 7. cxd4 { Knight to h6 now, sneaking toward f5. And d4 is not free: his queen watches it. [%cal Yg8h6,Rd1d4] } 7... Nh6 (7... Nxd4 { The pawn was poisoned! } 8. Nxd4 { A knight gone for a pawn. If your queen grabs back, his queen takes her. [%cal Rd1d4] }) (7... Nge7 { Just as good, honestly. } 8. Na3 Nf5 { Same dream square by the front door. }) (7... Bd7 { A tempo slower. } 8. Nc3 { The knight wanted f5 right now. }) (7... Bb4+ { The check achieves nothing. } 8. Nc3 { He blocks and develops. Your bishop must soon move again. }) 8. Nc3 { He develops. Finish the trip: knight to f5. [%cal Yh6f5] } 8... Nf5 { Beautiful. Three pieces lean on d4 and you are fully equal. He does the worrying now. [%cal Gf5d4,Gc6d4,Gb6d4] } (8... Bd7 { Too slow. The moment was now. } 9. Bxh6 gxh6 { Your king side is torn and the f5 dream is dead. White is clearly better. }) (8... Be7 { The bishop can wait. } 9. Bxh6 gxh6 { Same tear. The knight had to jump first. }) 0-1


[Event "French Defense: the bishop that blocks the queen"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
