{ Back to our Advance position, but now he tries the proud bishop move. Look sharp: pretty is not always safe. } 6. Bd3 { The bishop stands tall on d3, blocking his own queen's view of d4. How do you punish that? [%csl Rd3] } 6... cxd4 { Yes. Open the position first. } (6... Nh6 { The usual route, but he has a saving trade now. } 7. dxc5 Bxc5 { The d4 target is gone. You stand fine; the fun is smaller. }) (6... Bd7 { Right piece, wrong beat. } 7. dxc5 Bxc5 { Again the target vanishes. Trade pawns first and it cannot. }) (6... a6 { A useful wait, but he escapes. } 7. dxc5 Bxc5 { Target gone again. }) (6... c4 { Tempting. But the tension on d4 was your treasure. } 7. Bc2 { He tucks the bishop away and stands better. The c column play is gone. }) 7. cxd4 { Count the guards of d4: one knight, nothing more. But his bishop dreams of a check on b5. Cover that square. [%cal Rd3b5] } 7... Bd7 { Perfect. No more b5 tricks, and now knight takes d4 is a true threat. } (7... Nxd4 { One move too soon! } 8. Nxd4 Qxd4 9. Bb5+ { The check uncovers the d column. } 9... Bd7 10. Bxd7+ Kxd7 11. Qxd4 { And your queen is gone. Cover b5 first. }) (7... Nh6 { You are missing a gift! The bishop blocks the queen, so d4 hangs. } 8. Nc3 { He saves it. The chance has passed and he stands better. }) (7... Nge7 { The gift slips by. } 8. Nc3 { Now the grab fails: his knight would leap to b5, hitting queen and c7. }) 8. O-O { He castles. This is the Milner Barry gambit: the d4 pawn for open lines. Take it, right piece first. } 8... Nxd4 { The knight first. It can be traded off safely. } (8... Qxd4 { The wrong grabber! } 9. Nxd4 Nxd4 { Queen for a knight and a pawn. The knight had to take first. }) (8... Rc8 { You hesitated. } 9. Be2 { The bishop slinks home and d4 is guarded again. The moment was now. }) (8... Nge7 { Develop later. The pawn was ripe now. } 9. Nc3 { Guarded again, with b5 leaps in the air. }) (8... Be7 { The gift had a time limit. } 9. Be2 { Safe again. }) 9. Nxd4 { He recaptures. Now yes, the queen may take: the d3 bishop blinds his own lady. [%csl Rd3] } 9... Qxd4 { A clean pawn in the bag. } 10. Nc3 { His last trick: the knight arrives with b5 dreams. One tiny pawn move slams that door forever. Which one? } 10... a6 { Exactly. No knight to b5, no bishop to b5, not ever. A pawn up: develop and enjoy it. [%csl Ga6] } (10... Qxe5 { Greed number two. Now every white piece wakes up furious. } 11. Re1 Qb8 12. Nxd5 { And this knight is poison: open the e column and his rook crashes in. One pawn was plenty. }) (10... Qb6 { Safe, and still a pawn up. } 11. a3 { See, he plays the little pawn move himself. a6 at once was cleaner. }) (10... Bc5 { The bishop leaps into a storm. } 11. Be3 { He hits back with tempo and you are suddenly the one in trouble. }) 0-1


[Event "French Defense: the a3 sneak"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
