{ Sometimes he ducks the fight and swaps in the middle. A mirror, but only one of you brought a plan. } 3. exd5 { He trades. Take back with the pawn: queen stays home, shape stays clean. [%cal Ye6d5] } 3... exd5 (3... Qxd5 { The queen strolls out way too early. } 4. Nf3 { He develops for free while she looks for a home. Pawn takes was the move. }) 4. Nf3 { A mirror now. Copy calmly: knight to f6 first. [%cal Yg8f6] } 4... Nf6 (4... Nc6 { Fine as well. } 5. Nc3 { Just mind the bishop pin on b5 in these mirrors. }) (4... Bd6 { Good square. We simply bring knights before bishops. } 5. c4 { And see how the early poke asks questions at d5. }) (4... Be7 { A shade shy. } 5. Bd3 { d6 is the fighting square for that bishop. }) 5. Bd3 { Bishops out. Yours to d6, staring straight at his king side. [%cal Yf8d6] } 5... Bd6 (5... c5 { The French break works here too. Completely sound. } 6. c3 { It opens the game at once. Our line keeps the mirror one more beat. }) (5... Bg4 { Right idea, one move early. } 6. O-O { Castle first, then pin in comfort. }) (5... Be7 { Too modest. } 6. h3 { On d6 the bishop fights; on e7 it watches. }) 6. O-O { He castles. Copy: king safe before any fight. [%cal Ye8g8] } 6... O-O (6... Bg4 { The pin one beat too soon! } 7. Re1+ Be7 { The rook check drags your proud bishop back to block. Castle first. }) (6... Be6 { Solid enough. } 7. Nc3 { But the king was waiting his turn. }) (6... Nc6 { The knight takes c6 from your own pawn. } 7. Re1+ Be7 { And the e column check costs your bishop its post. }) 7. Bg5 { The first real question: he pins your knight. Answer in kind. Pin his! [%cal Yc8g4] } 7... Bg4 (7... Be6 { The engine's calm pick. Also excellent. } 8. Re1 { A long quiet game. The g4 pin bites harder. }) (7... h6 { You may poke first. } 8. Bh4 { The bishop steps back and keeps pinning. No harm either way. }) (7... c6 { Sturdy, but the pin was free. } 8. Nbd2 { He improves for nothing. }) (7... Nbd7 { Playable, though his c4 poke gets annoying soon. } 8. c4 { Pin first, then this knight. }) 8. Nbd2 { He breaks the pin politely. Copy once more: queen knight to d7. [%cal Yb8d7] } 8... Nbd7 { Done. Now the plans: c6 and queen c7, rook to e8, knight to f8 and g6. [%cal Bc7c6,Bf8e8] } (8... c5 { Breaking the mirror at once. The engine's favourite, and strong. } 9. h3 Bh5 { Keep the pin; a rich open game. Ours is the quieter road. }) (8... h6 { Fine. } 9. Bh4 { Everything holds. }) (8... Re8 { A good rook move too. } 9. h3 { The queen knight was itching first, but no harm. }) 0-1


