{ Now the sneaky knight to d2. He hides from the pin, but the hiding has a price: the knight is passive there. } 3. Nd2 { The Tarrasch. No pin can touch that knight. Develop yours: f6, hitting e4 anyway. [%cal Yg8f6] } 3... Nf6 (3... c5 { The engine's favourite, and a great line. } 4. Ngf3 Nc6 5. exd5 exd5 { An open world with free pieces, but a lot to learn. }) (3... dxe4 { Also sound. It melts into the trade line from the knight c3 chapter. } 4. Nxe4 { Same furniture: knight d7, trade, then c5. }) (3... Nc6 { The knight sits on your c pawn, and c5 is the French's lungs. } 4. c3 { He builds in peace; you stay cramped. Keep c6 free for the pawn. }) (3... a6 { A clever waiting system. Real, but its own course. } 4. Ngf3 { Today we keep it classic. }) (3... Be7 { Solid and slow. } 4. Ngf3 { The knight punch at e4 is more testing. }) 4. e5 { The pawn charges your knight. No panic: tuck it to d7, where it bites at e5. [%cal Yf6d7] } 4... Nfd7 { Snug. From d7 it leans on e5, and c5 comes next. [%cal Gd7e5] } (4... Ne4 { Brave, but nobody holds the knight's hand there. } 5. Nxe4 dxe4 { He just takes, and the stray pawn becomes a worry. The retreat kept it clean. }) (4... Ng8 { All the way home. Two moves for nothing. } 5. Ndf3 { He develops happily ahead. }) (4... c5 { Your knight! It is standing in the pawn's mouth. } 5. exf6 Qxf6 { A whole knight for a pawn. }) 5. Bd3 { The bishop takes the pretty diagonal. Now the punch you know: c5. [%csl Rd4][%cal Yc7c5] } 5... c5 (5... b6 { The poor French bishop dreams of a6. Real idea, wrong moment. } 6. c3 { Strike with c5 before anything. }) (5... f6 { Too soon. Your king side opens while nobody is home. } 6. Qh5+ { The queen crashes in at once. First c5, later f6. }) 6. c3 { He props d4, just like the Advance. Knight to c6: lean on it. [%cal Yb8c6] } 6... Nc6 (6... cxd4 { Fine. } 7. cxd4 Nc6 { Same house, different door. The knight and the f6 punch still come. }) (6... Qb6 { The Advance reflex! Playable, but here d4 is well fed. } 7. Ne2 { Our real key in this one is the f6 lever. }) 7. Ne2 { The clever square: f3 stays free for the other knight. Trade pawns now. [%cal Yc5d4] } 7... cxd4 (7... Qb6 { Fine. } 8. Nf3 { But see how well d4 is fed. The f6 lever is the door here. }) (7... f6 { Right idea, wrong order! } 8. Nf4 { Now this knight found a dream square, and e6 aches. Trade pawns first. }) 8. cxd4 { Locked again. So bring the second French lever: break with f6! [%csl Re5][%cal Yf7f6] } 8... f6 (8... Qb6 { The queen glares, but d4 holds. } 9. Nf3 { The f6 break is the door. }) (8... Be7 { Too quiet. Hit the chain while his king sits in the middle. } 9. O-O { He castles comfortably; your moment thins. }) 9. exf6 { He must take, or e5 falls. Recapture with the knight: it lands active. [%cal Yd7f6] } 9... Nxf6 (9... Qxf6 { The lady grabs. Natural, but she stands tall in the open wind. } 10. O-O { The knight belonged there, shielding your king. }) 10. Nf3 { See why e2 was clever? Now bishop to d6, the fighting post. [%cal Yf8d6] } 10... Bd6 (10... Bb4+ { The check trades your good bishop away. } 11. Bd2 { He happily offers the swap. Keep that bishop: d6 is its throne. }) (10... Be7 { Solid, but on d6 the bishop glares at his king side. } 11. O-O { A tempo shy of the best setup. }) 11. O-O { Kings must hide. Yours too: castle. [%cal Ye8g8] } 11... O-O { Your rook owns the open f column. If his bishop lands on f4, trade it, then knight to e4. [%cal Gf8f2,Bf6e4] } (11... Qc7 { Fighting spirit, but castle first. That open column cuts both ways. } 12. Nc3 { He gains time while your king waits in the draft. }) (11... Bd7 { Castle before lines open. } 12. Bf4 { He is first to the good squares. }) 0-1


