{ Last door: the knight to c3, his most popular move. Our answer is the simplest chess in the book. } 3. Nc3 { The knight guards e4. Take that pawn and trade the fuss away. [%cal Yd5e4] } 3... dxe4 (3... Nf6 { The Classical. Excellent and rich. } 4. Bg5 { The famous pin, and hours of homework. Our trade keeps the evening short. }) (3... Bb4 { The Winawer. Sharp, famous, double edged. } 4. e5 { Both kings run for their lives. Learn it someday; today we keep it clean. }) (3... Be7 { Too polite. } 4. e5 { He grabs space for free. }) (3... Nc6 { The knight sits on your c pawn. No c5, no French. } 4. e5 { He clamps, and your counterplay never comes. }) 4. Nxe4 { He recaptures. Now the key move of this line: knight to d7. It exists to take back on f6. [%cal Yb8d7] } 4... Nd7 (4... Nf6 { Natural, but watch your pawns. } 5. Nxf6+ gxf6 { Cracked. The d7 knight was born to take back here. }) (4... Bd7 { The fortress plan: bishop to c6 behind the wall. Solid and slow. } 5. Bd3 Bc6 { Playable. Ours frees the pieces faster. }) (4... Be7 { Quiet. } 5. Nf3 { The d7 knight plan is the smooth one. }) 5. Nf3 { He develops. Now the king knight: f6, offering the trade on your terms. [%cal Yg8f6] } 5... Ngf6 (5... Ndf6 { The wrong horse! This one exists to take back later. } 6. Ng3 { He dodges, and your knights trip over each other. }) (5... Be7 { Fine. } 6. Bd3 { The knight hop is more to the point. }) 6. Nxf6+ { He trades. Take back with the knight. That is the whole point of d7. [%cal Yd7f6] } 6... Nxf6 (6... Qxf6 { The queen takes, and becomes the target. } 7. Bg5 { With tempo! She runs again while he develops for free. }) (6... gxf6 { Your king side cracks for nothing. } 7. g3 { He will aim at the holes forever. The knight was placed to take back. }) 7. Bd3 { Pieces come off, plans remain. Hit his center: c5, the move you know by heart. [%cal Yc7c5] } 7... c5 (7... Bd7 { Equally good: the bishop aims at c6. } 8. O-O { We strike with the pawn first, while his center is tall. }) (7... b6 { Freeing the French bishop. Lovely idea, one beat late. } 8. c3 { He cements d4 and your c5 gets harder. }) (7... Be7 { Castle first chess. } 8. Qe2 { Fine, but c5 is the moment. }) 8. dxc5 { He swaps rather than defend. Take back with the bishop: it comes out fighting. [%cal Yf8c5] } 8... Bxc5 (8... Qc7 { You will win it back, but why wander? } 9. b4 { He clings on and gains space. Simpler to recapture at once. }) (8... Be7 { You get c5 back anyway. } 9. O-O { But the bishop stood prouder on c5 itself. }) 9. Bf4 { His bishop eyes c7. No fear: castle and stand tall. [%cal Ye8g8] } 9... O-O { Every piece free, no weaknesses, and the bad French bishop never happened. Well done. } (9... Qb6 { Just as strong: b2 and f2 both twitch. } 10. O-O { But b2 is bait with the rook behind it. Castling keeps life simple. }) (9... Nd5 { Poking the bishop. It just steps back. } 10. Bg3 { Nothing gained, and your knight left its post. }) (9... Bd7 { Fine, though his knight lands hard on e5 next. } 10. Ne5 { Castle first, kick later. }) 0-1
